auth-service

FastAPI gateway in front of phantom-daemon. Ed25519 JWT, Argon2id
passwords, TOTP MFA, RBAC, audit log, authenticated reverse proxy
to the daemon UDS.


v1.1.10 - 2026-11-05
--------------------

  Platform release. The image is built with every Debian package at
  the version Debian serves that day. The code, the schema and the
  configuration are unchanged.

  * Platform. The Dockerfile runs apt-get upgrade -y before it
    installs the packages. Packages the base image brings used to stay
    at the version of the base after Debian replaced them. Their
    security updates now reach the image, and the source of every
    installed package can be found when the image is built, which the
    manifest of the base images needs.


v1.1.9 - 2026-11-04
-------------------

  Proxy access release. The proxy forwards a fixed table of daemon
  routes instead of every path under /api, backup becomes superadmin
  only, and the request body limit is set per route. The trusted
  proxies follow the phantom-net subnets. Roles and the schema are
  unchanged.

  * Route table. routes/proxy.py lists the 26 daemon routes in ROUTES,
    matched exactly on method and path, and sends the daemon the
    table's path. A request outside the table is answered 404
    NOT_FOUND and audited without reaching the daemon. Before this
    release an admin reached /api/core/backup/export through spellings
    such as core/x/../backup/export or core/%2562ackup/export; nginx
    forwards them as written. Those spellings have no row now. A wrong
    method is 404 where the daemon answered 405.

  * Access levels. Each row names what the route asks of the caller:
    VIEW reads state and carries no client key, OPERATE changes VPN
    state or reads a client key, CUSTODY moves the whole data set.
    GRANTS maps an access to roles in one place: VIEW and OPERATE stay
    with admin and superadmin, CUSTODY is superadmin. A refusal is
    403 SUPERADMIN_REQUIRED, audited, before the body is read.

  * Body limits. AUTH_PROXY_MAX_BODY drops from 64 MiB to 64 KiB, and
    AUTH_PROXY_BACKUP_MAX_BODY carries 64 MiB for the two backup
    routes. The limit belongs to the route, not the role: a superadmin
    has the small limit everywhere else. nginx.conf in the daemon
    matches, with its own location for the backup routes.

  * Trusted proxies. PHANTOM_SUBNET_V4 and PHANTOM_SUBNET_V6 name the
    phantom-net subnets, the variables the daemon compose reads, and
    an empty value takes the compose default. AUTH_TRUSTED_PROXIES
    adds to them and no longer replaces them, so it is empty by
    default and cannot remove the subnets from the list.

  * Dependencies in routes. Depends() moves out of argument defaults
    into two Annotated aliases in middleware/auth.py, AuthPayload and
    SuperadminPayload. Route dependencies and the OpenAPI document are
    unchanged. app.py and routes/proxy.py keep their broad handlers
    and log the traceback. The rest of the tree follows ruff: imports,
    datetime.UTC, __slots__ order.

  No schema or migration impact. An admin loses backup in the panel
  until the dashboard reflects it; the bootstrap account is superadmin.


v1.1.8 - 2026-11-03
-------------------

  Platform release. Both images build their own platform from
  debian:trixie-slim, as the daemon's images do, and the interpreter
  moves from 3.12.13 to Debian trixie's 3.13.5. No code path changes.

  * The platform stage. Dockerfile and dev.Dockerfile start from
    debian:trixie-20261027-slim pinned by its index digest, install
    Debian's python3 with ca-certificates, netbase and tzdata, and
    create a venv at /opt/venv: Debian's python3 refuses pip installs
    into the system (PEP 668). apt, mount and the venv packages are
    then purged and the tree is copied into a single layer.
    requirements.txt installs into /opt/venv, which leads PATH, so
    `python -m auth_service` and tools/bootstrap.py run as before.
    On arm64 the production image is 53.1 MB in five layers, against
    60.4 MB in eight; it holds 83 Debian packages, each with its
    copyright file, and neither apt-get nor mount.

  * Python 3.13. requirements.txt already carried cp313 or abi3
    hashes for every compiled distribution on x86_64 and aarch64, so
    the lock is unchanged and the same 25 distributions install.
    auth_service imports no module 3.13 removed. One auth.db was
    carried through 3.12, 3.13, 3.12 and 3.13 images in turn; each
    time login, /auth/me and the audit log read the records the
    other interpreter had written.

  * THIRD_PARTY_LICENSES. generate_licenses.py resolves the inventory
    for 3.13 and the DEPENDENCIES table is unchanged. BUNDLED NATIVE
    CODE is remeasured on the cp313 x86_64 wheels. PyNaCl's row now
    reads 3627 KiB, the manylinux_2_34 wheel pip installs on trixie
    under either interpreter; the 5189 KiB it read before is the
    manylinux2014 wheel's extension. SYSTEM LIBRARIES AND BASE IMAGE
    names debian:trixie-20261027-slim and Debian's python3.13.

  No API, schema, or migration impact. The image needs a rebuild for
  the platform to take effect.


v1.1.7 - 2026-10-15
-------------------

  Licensing and packaging release. Adds the licence files the
  distribution never carried, makes them part of what the packager
  ships, and drops a system package nothing in the image linked.
  No code path changes.

  * LICENSE and THIRD_PARTY_LICENSES. LICENSE is AGPL-3.0 with the
    ARTEK İnovasyon and Rıza Emre ARAS copyright lines, matching the
    three vendor branches. THIRD_PARTY_LICENSES is generated by
    generate_licenses.py, which builds a throwaway environment from
    requirements.txt and runs pip-licenses (pinned 5.5.5) against
    it — two environments, kept apart because installing the tool
    beside the dependencies adds 34 distributions of its own to the
    inventory. 25 distributions are listed with the version, the
    SPDX identifier and the via graph uv already writes into
    requirements.txt as the Required By column. Nothing in the
    script classifies a licence: ACCEPTED is the policy (MIT,
    Apache-2.0, BSD-3-Clause, MPL-2.0, PSF-2.0) and the run stops on
    a package resolving to anything unlisted, on a declared string
    that is neither an SPDX identifier nor in the rewrite map, and
    on a package missing from LICENSE_URLS — so neither list can
    drift away from the dependency set. --check reports staleness
    without writing.

    Three packages needed a word in the header rather than a guess
    in the table: uvloop declares "MIT License" and ships
    LICENSE-APACHE and LICENSE-MIT side by side (MIT is the one
    taken); annotated-types declares no licence at all, so its entry
    comes from its classifier; and the file in certifi's wheel is
    the MPL notice block pointing at mozilla.org/MPL/2.0/, not the
    licence text, which is what MPL-2.0 section 3.1 asks a
    distributor to pass on. BUNDLED NATIVE CODE is written by hand
    for what distribution metadata cannot see: eight distributions
    ship a compiled extension, nine modules in all, each linking only
    glibc and GCC runtime libraries — whatever C or Rust they were
    built from is inside the extension.

  * The packager ships both files. scripts/packager.py's INCLUDE
    gained LICENSE and THIRD_PARTY_LICENSES, so dist/phantom-auth
    carries them at the package root alongside auth_service; 35
    files became 37. This is the first packaged version in which the
    consumer receives them.

  * libsodium23 left the image. The Dockerfile installed it and
    nothing linked it: PyNaCl's manylinux wheels have libsodium
    compiled in, and on both x86_64 and aarch64 the extension names
    only libpthread, libc and the loader as needed objects, with no
    libsodium.so anywhere in the file. An image built without the
    package passes Ed25519 sign and verify, Argon2id hash and
    verify, and imports auth_service.app, while dpkg -l libsodium23
    finds no record. It was the only package in that layer, so the
    apt invocation went with it and the image no longer runs apt at
    all. libsodium's ISC licence still travels inside the wheel, at
    licenses/licenses/LICENSE.libsodium.txt, and is listed under
    BUNDLED NATIVE CODE.

  No API, schema, or migration impact. The image needs a rebuild for
  the dropped layer to take effect.


v1.1.6 - 2026-09-26
-------------------

  Security maintenance release. Patches the bundled anyio runtime
  for two Snyk findings: CVE-2026-63374 (TLSStream.wrap resolves
  internationalized hostnames with IDNA 2003, so server_hostname
  can verify against a certificate whose SAN was encoded under
  IDNA 2008 — MitM on IDN targets) and CVE-2026-64847 (to_process
  leaves worker stderr on the parent pipe, where a blocking write
  deadlocks the worker). Neither path is exercised here: the proxy
  target defaults to a UDS, which carries no TLS hostname at all,
  and to_process appears nowhere in the service. anyio is pulled in
  transitively by httpx, starlette and watchfiles; the bump is
  surgical (4.12.1 -> 4.14.2) with no other pin moving. Lockfile
  regenerated via `uv pip compile --upgrade-package anyio`;
  requirements.in pins anyio explicitly, as it already does for
  starlette and idna.

  No API, schema, or migration impact.


v1.1.5 - 2026-07-17
-------------------

  Hardens the auth composition layer against an attacker who controls
  headers or knows half a credential. Three fixes, one discipline:
  client identity and the brute-force budget must both survive.

  * Client IP resolution behind trusted proxies. get_client_ip lived
    as two verbatim copies and both trusted X-Forwarded-For blindly,
    taking the leftmost entry — the one the client itself supplies.
    nginx appends to the incoming header, so a spoofed prefix
    survived the proxy, landed in the audit log, and keyed the login
    rate limiter — one fake IP per request made it decorative. Now a
    single implementation: the header is honored only when the peer
    is inside AUTH_TRUSTED_PROXIES (new env, comma-separated CIDRs;
    default is exactly the pinned phantom-net compose subnets), and
    entries walk right to left, skipping trusted hops — the first
    untrusted address is the client. A direct connection cannot
    spoof at all.
  * MFA endpoints joined the rate limit. The limiter guarded only
    /login; /mfa/verify and /totp/backup were unlimited, and a
    6-digit TOTP is brute-forceable. Worse, login reset the bucket
    on a correct password before the MFA branch — an attacker who
    knew the password could refill their own budget at will. Both
    endpoints now spend from the same per-IP budget (429
    RATE_LIMITED, audited), and the reset moved to the single
    success gate shared by all three auth paths: only completed
    authentication refills.
  * Superadmin password reset revokes the target's sessions, the
    discipline self-service change and delete_user already had. The
    audit entry now carries the target user_id.

  283 tests (21 new). No schema or migration impact; API impact is
  429 on the two MFA endpoints and session invalidation on admin
  password reset.


v1.1.4 - 2026-07-14
-------------------

  Base image hardening. Both the production and dev images move from
  the floating python:3.12-slim tag to a pinned
  python:3.12.13-slim-trixie — the exact interpreter patch is now
  nailed down (previously whatever 3.12.x the tag last resolved to)
  and the Debian front advances from bookworm to trixie (Debian 13),
  lowering the system-package CVE surface. libsodium23, the sole apt
  dependency, resolves cleanly on trixie (1.0.18). The Python minor
  stays 3.12, so the interpreter path and site-packages layout are
  unchanged and no source moves.

  No API, schema, or migration impact.


v1.1.3 - 2026-06-22
-------------------

  Security maintenance release. Patches the bundled starlette and
  idna runtimes for Snyk findings (starlette SSRF / unsafe reflection
  / name-resolution; idna ReDoS). The starlette bump (0.52.1 -> 1.3.1)
  is a semantic stability rename — starlette 1.0 was declared "no
  breaking changes from 0.x". FastAPI 0.135 declares
  `starlette>=0.46.0` with no upper bound; idna is transitive only.
  Lockfile regenerated via
  `uv pip compile --upgrade-package starlette --upgrade-package idna`;
  requirements.in pins the new floors explicitly.

  No API, schema, or migration impact.


v1.1.2 - 2026-05-16
-------------------

  Security maintenance release. Patches the bundled click runtime
  for CVE-2026-7246 (command injection via untrusted shell-quoted
  argument handling on Windows). click is pulled in transitively
  by uvicorn; the bump is surgical (8.3.1 -> 8.3.3) with no source
  or runtime behavior change. Lockfile regenerated via
  `uv pip compile --upgrade-package click`; requirements.in is
  untouched.

  No API, schema, or migration impact.


v1.1.1 - 2026-03-16
-------------------

  Single bump from v1.0.0; everything below shipped as part of this
  version. The work clusters into six logical milestones, written
  between v1.0.0 (Mar 6) and the version bump (Mar 16).

  RBAC, TOTP wizard, MFA hardening

    Introduces admin / superadmin roles enforced by require_auth
    and require_superadmin dependencies. The TOTP enrollment flow
    is rebuilt as a wizard with explicit setup → confirm steps.
    MFA paths are tightened — short-lived MFA token, backup codes
    are one-time and consumed atomically. The /api/* proxy is
    refactored alongside.

  2-step password change with safety net

    Password change splits into verify → change so the new password
    is never sent in the same request as the credential check.
    Setting the same password is rejected outright. A successful
    change revokes every active session for the user, forcing
    re-authentication across all clients.

  Audit log API + per-request audit

    A paginated GET /auth/audit is added (superadmin only) with
    filters and server-side sort that drive the Carbon DataTable on
    the dashboard side directly. Proxy requests are also
    audit-logged, so every daemon API call is attributable to a
    user.

  Error code system + docs

    The error surface is rewritten around an ApiException hierarchy
    plus a stable success_code pattern. Routes are modularised by
    concern (login / session / password / totp / users / audit /
    proxy). The error and env-var contracts get dedicated
    documentation (docs/API_CODES.md, docs/ENV_VARS.md).

  Thread safety + atomic audit pagination

    Audit pagination queries are first wrapped in a single read
    transaction, then collapsed into a single atomic query using
    COUNT(*) OVER() — eliminates the page-size race that could
    show duplicate or missing rows under concurrent inserts.

    The bigger win: SQLite connection management is rewritten as a
    per-request connection opened by an ASGI middleware. Sharing a
    single sqlite3 connection across asyncio workers was a real
    bug; the new model is sharing-free by construction.

  Binary response passthrough + body limit

    The proxy used to assume daemon responses were always JSON.
    Endpoints that return tarballs (backup export) now pass through
    raw, preserving content-type. The default proxy body limit is
    raised from 64 KB to 64 MB so the same backup endpoint can
    also accept large uploads on the inbound side.


v1.0.0 - 2026-03-06
-------------------

  Initial release. The complete authentication layer for
  phantom-daemon — bootstrap, login, MFA, session management, and
  the authenticated reverse proxy that fronts every daemon API
  call.

  * Ed25519 JWT signing and verification, manual implementation
    (no PyJWT dependency).
  * Argon2id password hashing via PyNaCl.
  * RFC 6238 TOTP with hashed one-time backup codes.
  * SQLite WAL-mode persistence for users, sessions, TOTP backup
    codes, and audit log.
  * tools/setup.sh + tools/bootstrap.py: one-shot Ed25519 keypair
    generation, auth.db creation, initial superadmin user with a
    32-char random password (printed once, never persisted in
    plaintext).
  * Authenticated reverse proxy for /api/{path:path} forwarding to
    the daemon over its Unix domain socket, with per-IP
    sliding-window rate limiting on /auth/login.
  * Cross-branch package workflow: builds dist/phantom-auth/ and
    commits the result into dev/daemon:services/auth-service/ as
    github-actions[bot] (see DISTRIBUTION).

  Same-day fixes folded into the release:

    * Ignore chmod failure on Docker-created secrets in CI.
    * Remove duplicate artifact upload from the package workflow.
    * Wire the dev/daemon push end-to-end.
    * Restore /api prefix in the proxy target URL (regression
      caught immediately after the initial commit).
